      usedToTreat: "Oral therapy for dermatophyte onychomycosis of toenails or fingernails; topical products treat selected tinea pedis, cruris or corporis and are not equivalent to oral nail therapy.",
      description: "Terbinafine is an allylamine antifungal that kills many dermatophytes by blocking squalene epoxidase, an early enzyme in fungal ergosterol synthesis. Ergosterol depletion destabilizes the membrane while intracellular squalene accumulates to toxic levels. Oral terbinafine concentrates in newly forming skin, hair and nail keratin, making it a major onychomycosis drug, but visible nail normalization lags for months as damaged nail grows out. Oral treatment can cause liver failure and prolonged or permanent taste/smell loss.",
      mechanism: "Fungal squalene epoxidase converts squalene to 2,3-oxidosqualene upstream of lanosterol and ergosterol. Terbinafine inhibits this enzyme noncompetitively, producing membrane sterol deficiency and fungicidal squalene accumulation. This target is not fungal CYP51, so terbinafine's inhibition of human CYP2D6 is an interaction property rather than the antifungal mechanism. Drug persists in keratinized tissue after plasma concentrations fall.",
