      description: "Calcipotriene is a synthetic vitamin D3 analog that treats plaque psoriasis by activating nuclear vitamin D receptors in keratinocytes. VDR-RXR transcription slows excessive epidermal proliferation and promotes more normal differentiation, directly opposing the rapid, immature scale production of a psoriatic plaque. It is not a corticosteroid and does not cause steroid skin atrophy, but excessive surface area or dose can increase systemic calcium absorption and cause hypercalcemia or hypercalciuria.",
      mechanism: "Calcipotriene binds VDR, which heterodimerizes with retinoid X receptor and binds vitamin-D response elements. Gene changes reduce keratinocyte proliferation, promote terminal differentiation and modulate T-cell/dendritic inflammatory signals. Systemically absorbed drug is rapidly converted to less active metabolites, yet large doses can still increase intestinal/bone/kidney calcium effects. Combining a corticosteroid attacks inflammation quickly while calcipotriene supports steroid-sparing normalization.",
